          "session_id": "full-mt-m-007",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I was told my thyroid is underactive.",
          "answer": "**What it means:** Hypothyroidism means the thyroid gland is not making enough thyroid hormone. The hormones (mainly thyroxine) set how fast many body tissues work. When levels run low, the pituitary gland in the brain releases more thyroid-stimulating hormone to try to drive the thyroid. That is why a blood test for this stimulating hormone is usually the first test. (Source: *Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)*, general clinical reference.)\n\n**How it is classified and tested:**\n- **Primary hypothyroidism** means the thyroid gland itself is the problem. It accounts for most cases. **Central hypothyroidism**, where the pituitary or hypothalamus is the problem, is uncommon.\n- The usual testing sequence is: a raised stimulating hormone result, then a free thyroxine measurement. Low free thyroxine with a raised stimulating hormone is **overt** hypothyroidism. A raised stimulating hormone with thyroxine still in the normal range is **subclinical** hypothyroidism. A thyroid antibody test can show whether an autoimmune process is the cause. (Source: *Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change*, endocrinology guidance.)\n- In the ICD-10-CM coding system, thyroid disorders sit in Chapter 4 (codes E00 to E07), which includes hypothyroidism. The retrieved material did not give the specific hypothyroidism subcode, so please check your record or a coding reference for that. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 4*.)\n\n**What I could not cover:**\n- **Lifestyle and day-to-day considerations:** My lifestyle lookup did not complete, so I have no sourced guidance on diet, activity, sleep or adherence. Please ask again later, or raise it with your clinician.\n- **Management in outline:** The passages I retrieved did not describe treatment options. Treatment decisions, including any medicine, dose or monitoring schedule, belong with your own clinician.\n\n**Next step:** If you have not already, ask the clinician who told you about this which test results led to the diagnosis (including whether it was overt or subclinical), and what follow-up testing they recommend. If you develop new symptoms that worry you, contact your clinician promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
