        {
          "schema_version": 2,
          "ts": "2026-10-14T03:04:45.413539Z",
          "trace_id": "6befbdf3cc0545ca99f8a7f2a5c42b7e",
          "session_id": "full-mt-m-007",
          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How often do they recheck it?",
          "answer": "I'm not sure who \"they\" is. It could be your clinician, a lab, or a guideline. The thyroid test (thyroid-stimulating hormone, or TSH) is the most likely subject, given your last message. If you meant something else, please tell me.\n\nIf you mean the thyroid test, the guidance says it is usually rechecked **six to eight weeks** after starting thyroid replacement treatment or after any dose change. It takes that long for the level to settle at a new steady point (*Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)*, general clinical reference). The same reference says the dose is set by the TSH level.\n\nThe interval varies for a few reasons:\n- **Settling time:** Changes take weeks to show up in the blood, so testing sooner would not give a reliable reading.\n- **Natural variation:** The hormone level shifts over the day, running higher overnight and early in the morning. It also dips during and after acute illness. A single reading can mislead, so the guidance says an abnormal result is usually repeated after several weeks before long-term treatment starts (*Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change*).\n\nThe sources I searched did not say how often TSH is checked once a dose is stable. Please ask your clinician about that schedule.\n\nIf you develop new chest pain or a racing or pounding heartbeat after starting or changing treatment, seek prompt medical assessment. If you have severe confusion, extreme drowsiness, or collapse, seek emergency care now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational tool and must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
